course: geography
lesson: 46
title: Volcanoes: how and where
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A volcano is an opening in the crust where melted rock, gas and ash escape from inside the Earth; the mountain is built by its own eruptions piling up around the vent.
explain: Underground the melted rock is called magma; once it pours out on the surface it is called lava.
explain: Open a fizzy drink that has been shaken: the gas rushes out and drags the drink with it; sticky magma full of gas erupts explosively the same way, while runny magma lets the gas escape and just pours out.
explain: Most volcanoes sit where plates pull apart or where one plate dives under another, and some, like Hawaii's, sit over a hotspot in the middle of a plate.
explain: Most volcanic activity happens unseen on the ocean floor, along the mid-ocean ridges where new crust is made.
fact: A volcano is an opening in the crust where melted rock, gas and ash escape from inside the Earth.
fact: Melted rock is called magma while it is underground and lava once it pours out on the surface.
fact: Most volcanoes sit along plate boundaries, either where plates pull apart or where one plate dives under another.
fact: Some volcanoes, like Hawaii's, are in the middle of a plate above a hotspot, a plume of hot rock rising from deep in the mantle.
fact: As the plate slides over a hotspot, a chain of volcanoes forms, and the Hawaiian islands get older the further they are from the hotspot.
fact: Shield volcanoes are wide and gently sloping, made of runny basalt lava that flows a long way.
fact: Stratovolcanoes are steep cones of layered lava and ash, and they often erupt explosively.
fact: The deadliest danger is a pyroclastic flow, an avalanche of hot gas, ash and rock racing downhill at over 100 km/h.
fact: Volcanoes release gases such as water vapour, carbon dioxide and sulfur dioxide, and their sulfur gases can smell of rotten eggs.
fact: Most of the Earth's volcanic activity happens unseen on the ocean floor along mid-ocean ridges.
fact: A volcano is usually called active if it has erupted in about the last 10,000 years, and extinct if it is not expected to erupt again.
fact: When a huge eruption empties the magma chamber, the ground above can collapse into a giant crater called a caldera.
exercise: What is a volcano? | An opening in the crust where melted rock, gas and ash escape | A mountain that is hollow inside, like an empty cone with nothing in it | A hill pushed up by an earthquake | A hole dug in the ground by a falling meteorite
exercise: What is melted rock called while it is still underground? | Magma | Lava rock | Volcanic ash | Basalt
exercise: What is melted rock called once it pours out on the surface? | Lava | Magma | Pumice | Granite
exercise: Where are most volcanoes found? | Along plate boundaries | In the middle of deserts | Exactly along the equator line | Near the North and South Poles
exercise: What is a hotspot? | A plume of hot rock rising from deep in the mantle under a plate | A very sunny place on the side of a volcano where the rocks get hot | A boundary where two plates collide | A crater full of hot water
exercise: Which famous volcanoes sit over a hotspot in the middle of a plate? | Hawaii's | Japan's | Iceland's on the ridge | The Andes volcanoes
exercise: What are shield volcanoes like? | Wide and gently sloping, made of runny basalt lava | Steep cones of lava and ash layers | Tiny cones only a metre tall, made of mud | Hollow towers of ice that melt in the summer sun
exercise: What are stratovolcanoes like? | Steep cones of layered lava and ash, often explosive | Wide flat shields with gentle slopes and very runny lava | Holes in the ground with no cone | Underwater ridges that never erupt
exercise: What is a pyroclastic flow? | An avalanche of hot gas, ash and rock racing downhill | A slow river of runny lava | A tall cloud of steam rising from a hot spring | A rain of cold water that falls just after an eruption
exercise: How fast can a pyroclastic flow move? | Over 100 km/h | About 1 km/h, a slow crawl | About 10 km a year | It does not move at all
exercise: Which gases do volcanoes release? | Water vapour, carbon dioxide and sulfur dioxide | Only pure oxygen, the same as trees | Helium and neon only | Only smoke from burning trees on the slopes
exercise: Where does most of the Earth's volcanic activity happen? | Unseen on the ocean floor along mid-ocean ridges | On the tallest mountains on land | In the hot deserts of Africa and Arabia | Under the thick ice of Antarctica and nowhere else
exercise: When is a volcano called active? | If it has erupted in about the last 10,000 years | Only if it is erupting right now, today | If it has ever erupted at any time in Earth's history | If it is taller than 1,000 metres
exercise: What is a caldera? | A giant crater left when the ground collapses into an emptied magma chamber | A small cone of ash that grows on the side of a volcano after every big eruption | A river of cooled lava | A hot spring high on a mountain
exercise: Why do some volcanoes explode while others just pour out lava? | Sticky magma traps gas until it bursts out; runny magma lets it escape | Some volcanoes are hotter than the Sun | Explosive ones are filled with sea water that boils and blows the top off | Explosive ones are much older
test: Ben says: "Lava and magma are two different kinds of rock." Put him right. | It's the same melted rock: magma underground, lava on the surface | He's right, magma is solid rock and lava is the liquid kind that flows | Magma is ash and lava is gas | Lava is the one underground and magma is the one on top
test: In a game, a character outruns a pyroclastic flow on foot. What's wrong? | Pyroclastic flows race at over 100 km/h, far faster than a runner | Nothing, they move at walking pace, so anyone fit can get away | They only ever move uphill, never down | They are made of cool water
test: "how come hawaii has volcanoes, it's not on a plate edge??" | It sits over a hotspot, hot rock rising from the deep mantle | It is right on the edge of the Pacific plate, where it meets Asia | Its volcanoes are made by the strong tides | It doesn't have any volcanoes really, just hills
test: The Hawaiian island of Kauai is old and worn, while Big Island still erupts. Why? | The plate carried Kauai away from the hotspot long ago | Kauai is closer to the hotspot than Big Island is, so it cooled first | The hotspot moves around the Pacific each year | Kauai was built by a different kind of volcano
test: Mia reckons most volcanic eruptions happen on mountains we can see. What's true? | Most happen unseen on the ocean floor along ridges | Most happen high in the Alps | Most happen in Britain's mountains | All of them are on land, where people can see and count them
test: What would happen if a huge eruption emptied a volcano's magma chamber? | The ground above could collapse into a caldera | The volcano would grow twice as tall | The volcano would turn into a lake of lava for ever | Nothing, the chamber stays empty and holds up
test: A wide, gently sloping volcano pours out runny lava that flows for kilometres. What type is it? | A shield volcano | A stratovolcano | A caldera | A hotspot ridge cone
test: A volcano hasn't erupted for 2,000 years. Tom says: "It's extinct, totally safe." Right? | No, it counts as active and could erupt again | Yes, 2,000 years means it is extinct | Yes, volcanoes only ever erupt once | No, it will certainly erupt next year
test: "the volcano stinks like rotten eggs, what is that" | Sulfur gases from the volcano | Lava burning old food in the crater | Rotting trees buried under the ash | The smell of the hot rocks only
test: A steep, cone-shaped volcano made of layers of ash and lava sits above a subduction zone. What is it most likely to do? | Erupt explosively from time to time | Pour out only gentle runny lava for ever | Never erupt again | Erupt only deep under the sea
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the distinction between magma and lava? | Magma denotes molten rock beneath the surface; lava denotes the same material once erupted | Magma denotes solid rock; lava denotes molten rock beneath the surface | Magma denotes volcanic ash; lava denotes the gas emitted during an eruption | The terms are applied to rocks of wholly different chemical composition and origin
exam: Identify the principal location of the majority of the Earth's volcanoes. | Along the boundaries of the lithospheric plates | Within the interior of the continents far from any boundary | Exclusively within the polar regions | Exclusively along the equator
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning a pyroclastic flow. | A current of hot gas, ash and rock descending at velocities exceeding one hundred kilometres per hour | A slow stream of basaltic lava advancing at walking pace | A sudden discharge of cold water from a crater lake upon the slopes of the volcano situated below | An emission of steam from a hot spring
exam: It is given that the magma chamber of a volcano has been evacuated by a major eruption. Ascertain the probable consequence for the overlying ground. | Its collapse to form a caldera | Its elevation to form a new stratovolcano | Its conversion to a mid-ocean ridge | Its separation from the plate as a hotspot
